<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josif Rajačić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1785–1861</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josif Rajačić was a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churchman, metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlovci</a> from 1842. At the May Assembly of Sremski Karlovci in 1848 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> elected him patriarch and proclaimed the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>. He sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> against the Hungarian revolution and later served as administrator of the Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeschwar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josif-rajacic.jpg" alt="Josif Rajačić" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Kriehuber (1800-1876) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josef_Rajacic_Litho_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
